    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 060421\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n921 PM PDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Shower and thunderstorm chances increase across the Mojave Desert \n  on Sunday as moisture surges northward.\n\n* Drier conditions expected for Labor Day, though monsoonal\n  moisture and warmer temperatures return midweek.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Friday.\n\nThe primary forecast concern involves a moisture surge from \nHurricane Marie, currently located several hundred miles off the \nsouthern tip of the Baja Peninsula. Southwesterly flow aloft over \nthe southwestern United States will draw moisture from the system \ninto the region, bringing PWATs into the 1.0 to 1.5 inch range. The \napproach of a 500 mb perturbation in the flow pattern supports the \ndevelopment of a band of precipitation stretching southwest to \nnortheast along the Interstate 15 corridor. However, a slight shift \nto the north or south could bring much higher precipitation amounts \nto otherwise dry areas. Widespread cloud cover and the early morning \ntiming of the mid-level perturbation support widespread stratiform \nprecipitation rather than convective thunderstorms. Flow \nperpendicular to certain mountain ranges is an excellent setup for \norographic lift and the greatest precipitation totals are expected \nover high terrain. On the Spring Mountains, there is a 70 percent \nchance or greater of one inch of precipitation over 24 hours over \n8,000 feet in elevation and a 30 percent chance of two inches of \nprecipitation. Totals in the Las Vegas Valley will be far lighter, \nbut there is a 20 to 40 percent chance of at least one quarter inch \nof rain in 24 hours. Roadway ponding and running washes are possible \nacross much of the Mojave Desert. Precipitation should end on Sunday \nnight as the disturbance moves inland.\n\nWarmer and drier conditions are expected on Labor Day as heights \naloft start to increase again. High pressure will continue to build \nand move west through the workweek. The position and strength of the \nhigh will affect how much moisture is is able to draw into the \nwestern United States, from both tropical systems and traditional \nmonsoonal sources. Temperatures are also dependent on precipitation \nand cloud cover, but rising heights aloft should generally send \nhighs back towards normal values for early September.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package... \nSouthwest winds of 10 knots or less are expected through the \novernight period. Expect gradually increasing cloud cover through \nthe night with bases lowering to less than 10kft AGL by mid-morning. \nShower chances will develop as early as 12Z, but will become more \nlikely after 15Z, with the potential for light to moderate rain from \n15Z through 00Z. CIGs will generally remain between 7kft and 9kft \nAGL, but could drop to around 5kft AGL at times during the \nafternoon, along with a 10 percent chance of visibility reductions \nto less than 6SM. Isolated convection is also possible, mainly at \nthe onset of precipitation. Showers will end Sunday evening, with \ngradually improving ceilings overnight into Monday morning. \nTemperatures will remain below 100F through at least Tuesday.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Expect gradually \nincreasing cloud cover overnight from southwest to northeast, with \nCIGs falling to 8kft to 12kft by morning for all the TAF sites \nexcept KBIH, where SCT high clouds are expected. Shower chances will \nstart to increase after 12Z across the western Mojave Desert and by \n15Z across southern Nevada. CIGs will generally remain around 10kft \nAGL, but may lower to between 4kft and 6kft AGL with some heavier \nshowers through the day. There is also a 10% chance of visibility \nreductions to less than 6SM. Isolated areas of convection are also \npossible, especially at the onset of precipitation. The \nprecipitation will end from southwest to northeast Sunday evening, \nwith gradually improving ceilings overnight into Monday morning. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Meltzer\nAVIATION...Planz\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
